{/*
  ── THE ONE VIEW TRIGGER, AND WHY IT IS INLINE RATHER THAN THE SHARED MODULE ──

  `243:3` gives M-P2 and M-P3 the same trigger: IntersectionObserver at 25% of
  the section in view, once. src/scripts/motion.ts already implements exactly
  that and the homepage imports it, so importing it here is the obvious and
  architecturally correct thing to do.

  IT COSTS THE PAYLOAD BUDGET, AND THAT BUDGET IS ZERO. Astro inlines a bundled
  <script> only while it resolves to one chunk with nothing shared. With one
  importer (the homepage) that holds and the build ships 0 JS files, which
  specs/architecture/PERFORMANCE-BUDGETS.md records as a measured competitive
  asset and a deliberate ratchet: "the moment a bundle is legitimately needed,
  the budget changes by an explicit decision recorded as an ADR, rather than by
  a dependency arriving unnoticed." Measured on a scratch build, adding these
  two pages as importers splits the module out and emits four files, 1.45 KB.
  Small, and still exactly the unnoticed arrival the budget exists to catch.

  THE REAL FIX IS THREE LINES IN layouts/Base.astro: call initMotion() there
  once, so every route shares ONE entry, Astro inlines it again, the file count
  returns to zero, the cursor light and the per-section thresholds come back,
  and this block and its twin delete. src/layouts/** is outside this pass, so it
  is REPORTED rather than done, and what follows is the interim: the trigger and
  nothing else.

  NO try/catch AND NO FAILSAFE TIMER. Every call is feature-guarded so nothing
  here can throw, and there is nothing to rescue: the rest state of every
  animation on this page IS its final state. If this never runs, the page is the
  finished drawing.

  THIS COMMENT IS A JSX COMMENT AND IS COMPILED AWAY. A comment inside an
  is:inline script ships to every reader, and 2 KB of reasoning on a route
  already at 97 KB against a 100 KB budget is a real cost. The argument belongs
  in the source; the shipped script is four guards and an observer.
*/}
<script is:inline>
  /* The view trigger. See the note above this tag. */
  (function () {
    if (!window.matchMedia || window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches) return;
    if (typeof IntersectionObserver !== 'function') return;
    var els = document.querySelectorAll('[data-lit]');
    if (!els.length) return;
    var io = new IntersectionObserver(function (entries, self) {
      for (var i = 0; i < entries.length; i++) {
        if (!entries[i].isIntersecting) continue;
        entries[i].target.setAttribute('data-lit', 'on');
        self.unobserve(entries[i].target);
      }
    }, { threshold: 0.25 });
    for (var j = 0; j < els.length; j++) io.observe(els[j]);
  })();
</script>

<style>
  /* ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     GEOMETRY ONLY, THROUGHOUT.

     Not one border-radius, shadow, card fill, timing value or colour is
     authored in this file. Panels are `.sv-raised`, cards are `.surface`,
     figures are `.sv-numeral`, the floor step is `.sv-floor`, and every
     duration is --m-beat or --m-cycle. Where a rule below does set a radius it
     is a chip or a pill from the radius scale, never a card.
     ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */

  .row { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.75rem; justify-content: center; }
  .row--after { margin-top: var(--sec-gap); }

  .hint {
    margin: var(--stack-1) 0 0;
    text-align: center;
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-micro);
    color: var(--c-text-muted);
  }

  .kicker {
    margin: 0 0 var(--stack-1);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--t-micro);
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--c-text-muted);
  }

  /* ── The section wrapper and its ambient rig ─────────────────────────────
     `isolation: isolate` so the z-index -1 field cannot escape behind the page
     background, and `position: relative` so the rig has something to measure
     against. Identical in construction to the six homepage sections. */
  .cw {
    position: relative;
    isolation: isolate;
  }

  /*
   * ── M-P5 · AMBIENT FIELD, SCROLL DRIVEN, NEVER SCROLL JACKING ────────────
   *
   * TRIGGER   @supports (animation-timeline: view()) only, via the shared
   *           layer in styles/tokens.css. Nothing is pinned, nothing snaps,
   *           and the scroll rate is untouched.
   * CADENCE   tied to scroll, linear against scroll position, plus the
   *           always-on --m-drift breath on the fields themselves.
   * REDUCED   the whole block is dropped: tokens.css declares both the drift
   *           and the parallax inside prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference,
   *           so the static bloom below is all that ships, and it is already
   *           correct.
   * NO SUPPORT the static field, which is what Firefox Release gets today.
   *
   * DIVERGENCE FROM `243:3`, RECORDED. The board asks for three layers at
   * 0.94x, 1.00x and 1.06x of scroll WITHIN one section. The platform gives one
   * --par per rig and phases the fields inside it, and its own comment says
   * "--par IS THE PER-SECTION SPEED. Sections set it to differ from one
   * another, which is what stops seven layers moving as one sheet." So the
   * differential is spent ACROSS the five rigs below rather than inside each
   * one. Same effect on a scrolling reader, and no second parallax mechanism.
   *
   * The bleed is exactly one gutter: enough to reach the viewport edge rather
   * than drawing a seam one gutter in from it, and never one pixel more,
   * because the sitewide gate fails any page whose scrollWidth exceeds its
   * clientWidth at 390, 768 or 1440.
   *
   * `closest-side` on the mask, not a percentage pair, so the ramp reaches
   * transparent exactly at the middle of every edge and past it at every
   * corner. A percentage ellipse leaves about 25% of the field alive at the
   * edge midpoints, which draws a lighter rectangle across the section.
   */
  .amb {
    position: absolute;
    inset-block: -14%;
    inset-inline: calc(-1 * var(--gutter));
    z-index: -1;
    overflow: hidden;
    pointer-events: none;
    -webkit-mask-image: radial-gradient(closest-side, rgb(0 0 0) 42%, transparent 100%);
    mask-image: radial-gradient(closest-side, rgb(0 0 0) 42%, transparent 100%);
  }

  .amb b {
    position: absolute;
    border-radius: 50%;
    filter: blur(150px);
    mix-blend-mode: screen;
  }

  .amb b:nth-child(1) {
    width: 560px;
    height: 440px;
    left: -8%;
    top: -22%;
    background: var(--orb-teal);
    opacity: 0.42;
  }

  .amb b:nth-child(2) {
    width: 540px;
    height: 440px;
    right: -8%;
    bottom: -24%;
    background: var(--orb-violet);
    opacity: 0.34;
  }

  /* The per-section parallax speeds. Five values, deliberately unequal, so the
     rigs never travel as one sheet. */
  .cw--hero .amb { --par: 48px; }
  .cw--mech .amb { --par: 34px; }
  .cap--art-right .amb { --par: 26px; }

  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .amb b { filter: blur(90px); width: 320px; height: 280px; }
  }

  /* ══ THE HERO PLANE STACK, `223:5` ═══════════════════════════════════════ */

  /* --sec-pad-y, not --sec-gap, and the reason is measurable: the two ghost
     planes are positioned at -13% and -7% of the stack's own height, which at
     the height this artefact settles to is about 65px ABOVE the front plane.
     At one --sec-gap of clearance they landed on the price hint above them. */
  .duty {
    position: relative;
    margin: var(--sec-pad-y) auto 0;
    max-width: var(--measure-band);
  }

  /* The two planes behind. They hold nothing, so they are pure geometry: the
     same silhouette, offset up and out, each occluded by the one in front.
     Occlusion is what makes this depth rather than a drawn suggestion of it,
     which is why the front plane is opaque. */
  .duty__ghost {
    position: absolute;
    inset-inline: 6% -4%;
    border-radius: var(--radius-card);
    border: 1px solid var(--c-border);
    background: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--c-raised), var(--c-floor));
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-ladder);
  }

  .duty__ghost--far {
    top: -13%;
    height: 46%;
    opacity: 0.5;
  }

  .duty__ghost--mid {
    top: -7%;
    height: 52%;
    opacity: 0.75;
  }

  /* Padding only. The radius, the hairline, the 5% wash, the opaque body and
     the five-layer ladder are all `.sv-raised` in styles/surfaces.css.
     Restating any of them would put this file back among the twenty-one card
     recipes the audit found, and an unlayered scoped rule would win, silently
     cancelling the opaque step. */
  .duty__face {
    position: relative;
    padding: var(--blk-pad);
  }

  /* THE TILT. See the note at the call site for the bounding-box arithmetic.
     A single transform on the wrapper, so the three planes stay one object. */
  @media (min-width: 1100px) {
    .duty { transform: rotate(5deg); }
  }

  .art__kicker,
  .duty__kicker {
